"product":{"label":"Ospen 500 mg film-coated tablets","url":"https://medikamente.basg.gv.at/documents/1-18782__DOTC_FACH_INFO.pdf","source":"BASG"},"groups":[{"ruleId":"beta_lactam","name":"β-lactam ring","atoms":[1,6,8,11,12],"ring":[12,11,8,6],"count":1,"term":"beta-lactam","note":{"caption":"Four-membered cyclic amide that locks onto the enzymes building the cell wall.","body":["A β-lactam is an amide whose nitrogen and carbonyl carbon both sit in a four-membered ring. Penicillins use it to bind covalently to penicillin-binding proteins, enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan at the last stage of cell-wall assembly.","An active-site serine ends up attached to the opened ring as an inactive acyl-enzyme. β-Lactamases defend bacteria by hydrolysing this ring, which inactivates the drug."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. β-lactam (CHEBI:35627)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:35627","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Bush K, Bradford PA. β-Lactams and β-lactamase inhibitors: an overview. Cold Spring Harb Perspect Med 2016;6(8):a025247","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4968164/","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"thiazolidine","name":"Thiazolidine ring","atoms":[0,6,8,9,10],"ring":[0,9,10,6,8],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Five-membered sulfur ring fused to the β-lactam: the penam core every penicillin shares.","body":["In penicillins the β-lactam is fused to a five-membered thiazolidine ring, giving the bicyclic penam nucleus. Every penicillin carries two methyl groups at position 2 and a carboxylate at position 3 of this core.","What tells one penicillin from another is the group attached at position 6."],"sources":[{"citation":"Tooke CL, Hinchliffe P, Bragginton EC, et al. β-Lactamases and β-lactamase inhibitors in the 21st century. J Mol Biol 2019;431(18):3472-3500","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723624/","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. penicillin (CHEBI:17334)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:17334","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"carboxylic_acid","name":"Carboxylic acid","atoms":[2,3,10,15],"ring":[],"count":1,"term":"ionisable","note":{"caption":"Position 3 carboxyl of the penicillin core; losing its proton gives the anion.","body":["The carboxyl group at position 3 is part of the definition of a penicillin. Loss of its proton gives a penicillinate anion, the negatively charged form.","Enzymes that attack penicillins use this group as a handle: in zinc-dependent β-lactamases, structural studies describe the substrate carboxylate interacting with an active-site zinc ion."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. penicillin (CHEBI:17334)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:17334","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. penicillinate anion (CHEBI:51356)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:51356","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Tooke CL, Hinchliffe P, Bragginton EC, et al. β-Lactamases and β-lactamase inhibitors in the 21st century. J Mol Biol 2019;431(18):3472-3500","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6723624/","licence":"CC BY 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"amide","name":"Amide","atoms":[4,7,16,17],"ring":[],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"Carboxamido link at position 6 that carries the phenoxyacetyl side chain.","body":["The side chain joins the penam nucleus through an amide at position 6. In penicillin V the acyl part is a phenoxyacetyl group; in other penicillins a different group sits here.","The closely related penicillin G has been described as structurally similar to the terminal D-Ala-D-Ala of the growing peptidoglycan, the part the target enzymes work on."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. phenoxymethylpenicillin (CHEBI:27446)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:27446","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"ChEBI. penicillin (CHEBI:17334)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:17334","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Bush K, Bradford PA. β-Lactams and β-lactamase inhibitors: an overview. Cold Spring Harb Perspect Med 2016;6(8):a025247","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4968164/","licence":"Free full text"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}},{"ruleId":"aryl_ether","name":"Aryl ether","atoms":[5,17,18],"ring":[],"count":1,"note":{"caption":"The phenoxy oxygen that makes this penicillin V rather than penicillin G.","body":["The phenyl ring is joined to the side chain through an ether oxygen, giving the phenoxyacetyl group that defines penicillin V. Penicillin V is described as relatively stable to gastric acid.","The sources used here record that acid tolerance but do not explain which structural feature is responsible, so this note does not assign it to the ether."],"sources":[{"citation":"ChEBI. phenoxymethylpenicillin (CHEBI:27446)","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/searchId.do?chebiId=CHEBI:27446","licence":"CC BY 4.0"},{"citation":"Rawson TM, Wilson RC, Moore LSP, et al. Exploring the pharmacokinetics of phenoxymethylpenicillin (penicillin-V) in adults: a healthy volunteer study. Open Forum Infect Dis 2021;8(12):ofab573","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8684501/","licence":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0"}],"review":{"status":"unreviewed"}}}],"pubchemUrl":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/6869"}